#!/usr/bin/python3
"""Keeps the TPM opening the encrypted disk by itself across kernel
switches and Secure Boot database updates.

The TPM gives the disk key back only if PCR 7 holds the value it was
bound to. PCR 7 records the Secure Boot variables (SecureBoot, PK, KEK,
db, dbx) and the certificates that verified each boot binary, so
booting the Raye kernel (verified with the Raye key from MokList) or
writing a new dbx changes it. This tool reads the firmware event log,
replays PCR 7, checks the replay equals the TPM, and predicts the value
of the next boot. It then adds a clevis binding sealed to that value
(pcr_digest), keyed by the passphrase of a binding that still opens
the disk. The key only goes through pipes, never to disk.

Before a change it cannot predict (a firmware update that has not
written its variables yet, a new shim or GRUB), the TPM protection is
suspended for one start: a binding with no PCR policy is added, and the
first start after it seals the disk to the PCR 7 that start really
measured, then removes that binding.

    raye-tpm replay [--bank B]           event log -> PCR 7, compared with the TPM
    raye-tpm predict --kernel K [--bank B]
    raye-tpm bind-next --kernel K        bind every TPM disk for the next boot of K
    raye-tpm prepare-firmware [--if-changed]
                                         suspend for one start, and bind for every
                                         kernel from the current Secure Boot variables;
                                         --if-changed: only if they differ from what
                                         this boot measured (raye-tpm-watch.service)
    raye-tpm suspend                     open the disk without PCR 7 for one start
    raye-tpm resume                      after that start: seal to PCR 7 again and
                                         remove the suspension (raye-tpm-check.service)
    raye-tpm record                      tag the bindings that open the disk now
    raye-tpm status [--json]             per disk: which bindings open it now
    raye-tpm prune                       remove bindings no kernel can use

K is "raye" or "ubuntu", B is "sha256" or "sha1". On any doubt (the
log does not replay to the TPM's value, an event it cannot recompute)
nothing is changed and the disk asks its password once, as before.

Exit codes: 0 done, 1 error, 2 prediction refused (nothing changed),
3 nothing to do here (no TPM, no event log, no disk bound to the TPM,
nothing suspended), 4 a disk could not be bound, suspended or pruned.
"""

